                <pb facs="0732" n="724" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg724"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/1036: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Butrick</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-07-27T11:00:00+09:00">July 27, 1940—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 11:40 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>701. The Inspector General of Customs has handed to me a letter in which he
                    states that he feels that the present Japanese pressure to obtain stronger
                    control over customs establishments will if unchecked jeopardize the stability
                    of the inspectorate and that foreign support is more than ever essential at this
                    time. He feels that public reiteration of the American attitude regarding the
                    integrity of the Customs would be advisable. I informed him orally that I would
                    bring his views to the attention of the Department.</p>
                <p>Sent to Department. Repeated to Chungking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Butrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
